  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 156 PM EDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nAfternoon water vapor imagery reveals quite nicely a compact \nmidlevel low spinning over the Plains, centered near Sioux Falls, \nthough high pressure centered north of Lake Superior is helping to \nsuppress this well south of the UP. Closer to home, we remain under \npatchy high cloud cover, with temperatures so far only in the mid \nand upper 50s. With more breaks in the cloud cover developing, most \nspots should be able to climb slightly higher into the lower 60s. \nThe exception will be over the Keweenaw and the north-central UP, \nwhere northeast flow off of Superior should limit temperatures to \nthe mid/upper 50s. Tonight, skies gradually clear from north to \nsouth, and with light winds allowing for good radiational cooling, \nwe are looking at another chilly night. Temperatures this past night \nbottomed out in the mid 30s across much of the interior-UP, with \nsome of our typically cooler spots below freezing. Given clearing \nskies, we stand a better chance at dropping even further with lows \nranging in the lower to mid 30s for most of the UP and possibly \nflirting with the upper 20s. Will be issuing frost/freeze headlines \nfor most of the UP, excluding the Keweenaw, late tonight into early \nTuesday. \n\nThe Ontario high pressure center strengthens and tracks east \ntomorrow, setting up a Hudson Bay High pattern to the northeast with \na Plains trough immediately to the southwest. As the surface high, \nreinforced by mid-level flow, strengthens into Wednesday, the Plains \ntrough moves south toward the Lower Midwest, and clear skies prevail \nby the second half of tomorrow. Modeled soundings show deep-layer \ndry air with substantial (>20C) surface dewpoint depressions Tuesday \nafternoon, yielding minimum RHs in the high 20s in the western \ninterior (widespread 30s elsewhere) tomorrow afternoon. Dry weather \npersists Wednesday and Thursday, though more moisture should be \nworking into the area as the surface high shifts eastward and \nsoutherly flow develops over the UP; expect RH to bottom out around \n35-40% in the driest spots Wednesday, and to remain above 40% area-\nwide Thursday. Still, even Tuesday, critical fire weather conditions \nwill be far from achieved given light winds <10 knots in the driest \nspots and high temperatures in the low 60s for the next few days.\n\nThe split flow pattern slowly moves east of the Great Lakes region \ninto Thursday as high pressure persists over Ontario/Quebec into the \nweekend. Slight variations in LREF clusters make their appearance \nbeginning on Saturday, general consensus exists that some form of \nregional ridging is likely through Sunday. Thus, below-average high \ntemperatures gradually warm into above-average high temperatures (5-\n10 above on Saturday) by the weekend while conditions remain dry.",
